        "name": "Eric R",
        "username": "Eric_R",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/e/b9bd4f/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2018-08-21T21:45:06.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Ted (paraphrased nicely by jffdougan above) explained some of the mechanical reasons that making a low-complexity Spirit is difficult. His lists of requirements for Low/Medium/High complexity aren't hard-and-fast - I don't agree with them 100% - but they're decent litmus tests. Also, there's the simple fact that Low-complexity Spirits tend to have many fewer mechanical knobs to tweak / ways they can be tweaked, so balancing them can be a lot harder.</p><p>However, there's another reason low-complexity Spirits are hard, which is that most interesting Spirit concepts can be better thematically represented as a Medium- or High-complexity Spirit. It kind of...&nbsp;<em>hurts?...</em> on an artistic level to wedge them into a low-complexity design.</p><p>There are exceptions: some Spirits fit nicely into that niche without too much effort. But the ones which don't... never quite settle down. Shadows Flicker Like Flame is like this; its design never really wanted to be Low complexity, and some people find its theming a bit scattershot as a result - but doing it \"right\" would have totally blown its complexity budget.</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:52:41.313Z",
